## R11. Concrete technical approaches — research extension, 2026-09-28

This extension turns R2–R8 into a ranked set of implementable mechanisms. Source inspection still targets `2f272c73cace4f4c111fd0cb2d4f9dcc60be2aaa`. “Exact” below means exact for a stated mathematical objective or preserved operation order; it does not mean that a different algorithm preserves current product outputs. No new model/library has been installed and no production implementation is included in this revision.

**TECH-00 — Two adoption routes.** A quality-changing representation, objective, clustering rule or recognizer must satisfy EVAL-06 in full. A behavior-preserving optimization instead needs a defined equivalence domain, an independent output oracle, identical consumer-visible decisions, and a material measured resource benefit under the frozen workload/budget. It need not invent a positive quality gain over identical output. Approximate numerics require an explicit error budget and decision-boundary checks; if these reveal changed outputs, classify the candidate as quality-changing. Neither route may weaken recurrence, coverage, privacy, input identity or cancellation requirements. Kernel measurements below nominate experiments; browser measurements decide adoption.

### TECH-01. Fuse passage comparisons and remove redundant pooling work

**Direct fit:** `shared/analysis/importance.ts`, `vector.ts`, and the installed Transformers.js `pipelines/feature-extraction.js` / `utils/tensor.js`.

For a topic with n uniquely identified passages, current novelty uses `n(n−1)/2` cosines and recurrence uses `n(n−1)`. Compute `s=max(0,cosine(x_i,x_j))` once for every `i<j`; add it to both recurrence sums and update passage j's maximum similarity to earlier passages. Return recurrence sum divided by `n−1`; novelty is `1−earlierMax`, with first-passage novelty still 1. This reduces pairwise cosine evaluations from `3n(n−1)/2` to `n(n−1)/2`, with O(n) additional accumulators and no similarity matrix. It remains O(n²d), so chunk/yield and cancellation are still necessary.

Retain the existing `cosine` implementation and ascending pair traversal first: recurrence additions can occur in the same per-passage order as today. If duplicate passage IDs are accepted, preserve the existing exclusion-by-ID recurrence denominator; novelty still considers earlier duplicate IDs. Do not silently change those semantics. Only later test precomputed norms or SIMD as separate numerical changes. The convenient centroid-sum recurrence shortcut is **not generally equivalent** because recurrence clamps every pair at zero.

A second independent kernel opportunity is mean pooling. Installed code walks embedding dimensions outside tokens and repeatedly converts the same attention-mask element. Walk tokens outside dimensions, convert each mask once, accumulate into Float64 scratch space, then cast the averaged vector to the existing output dtype and normalize as before. This makes hidden-state reads contiguous while preserving accumulation order for each dimension. Do not patch `node_modules`; benchmark an owned adapter/upstream change with the pinned dependency. Reject zero-valid-token/nonfinite inputs at the established validation boundary rather than inventing values.

**Checked evidence:** native-realm Node 24 prototypes preserved all six ranking signals in 60 fixtures and float32 pooling output in 18 fixtures. They exercised empty/singleton ranking, mixed-sign/positive/zero vectors, duplicate IDs, different widths and padding. R12 records timings and limits. Add long-topic equivalence, cancellation, Unicode keyword/discourse fixtures and full consumer-output checks before implementation is accepted. These are the best initial efficiency candidates because they do not require new weights or new topic labels.

### TECH-02. Batch by token length; evaluate an ONNX graph that returns pooled vectors

**First, eliminate padding waste without changing text.** Tokenize with the packaged tokenizer and exact prefix/special-token policy. Bucket bounded groups of windows by their actual token length, retain original indices, embed with the existing batch size 32, then scatter vectors back into chronological order before any boundary calculation. Never concatenate unrelated windows into one model sequence to “pack” them. Preserve every source mapping, the short tail and cancellation/progress accounting. A bounded reorder buffer prevents an entire library of text from becoming an in-memory batch. Any variable batch-size experiment requires a separate amendment to the existing batch-32 contract.

A dense-attention work proxy is `sum_batches(batchCount * maxTokenLength²)`; projection/feed-forward work also matters, so this is not a runtime predictor. In a deliberately skewed arithmetic example (eight batches, each with 31 lengths of 32 and one of 512), regrouping reduces that proxy by about 7.79× at unchanged batch size. **This is a toy cost calculation, not measured inference acceleration.** Verify batched versus original vectors and topic decisions on RU/UK/EN text; padding shape may change numerical kernels. The current feature-extraction path explicitly enables padding and truncation, so UNIT-02 must land first.

**Then test reducing model-output traffic.** The installed pipeline reads token states and pools/normalizes them in JavaScript. Inspect the actual packaged ONNX output signature and profile this cost. A separately fingerprinted export could place masked mean and L2 normalization inside the graph:

```text
u = sum_t(mask_t * hidden_t) / sum_t(mask_t)
v = u / ||u||₂
```

Match current inclusion of prefix/special tokens, mask handling, dtype casts and zero-vector policy. Expose `[batch,384]` instead of `[batch,tokens,384]` to JavaScript. For float32 batch 32 and 256 tokens, that output payload is 12 MiB versus 48 KiB, a **256× output-size reduction**, not a 256× model-memory or latency gain. Internal transformer activations still exist. A pooled-only graph needs an explicit worker adapter; do not run the existing token-pooling code over its already-pooled output. Treat the changed graph hash as a new embedding artifact and validate Q8/backend parity near decision thresholds. [ONNX export/pooling boundary](https://www.sbert.net/docs/sentence_transformer/usage/efficiency.html), [ORT GPU tensor ownership](https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/tutorials/web/ep-webgpu.html)

Do not enable graph capture globally: it requires suitable static shapes and supported GPU execution. Dynamic length buckets, final partial batches, extra sessions and retained GPU buffers can erase its benefit. WASM threads require verified cross-origin isolation and a capture-contention experiment; the current single-thread fallback stays until that separate spike passes. [ORT performance conditions](https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/tutorials/web/performance-diagnosis.html)

### TECH-03. Reuse exact encoder inputs, while keeping timeline metadata revision-specific

**Concrete cache decomposition:** one record maps an exact ordered token sequence plus its attention-mask semantics and encoder/artifact/preprocessing fingerprint to a vector; a separate manifest maps the current transcript generation to ordered vector keys and current time/speaker/source-span metadata. Use canonical length-delimited serialization and a collision-resistant digest; retain enough manifest data to validate hits. Never key reusable vectors solely by `recordingId`, raw unprefixed text, or a segment index.

Timestamp/speaker corrections can reuse text-only vectors when the actual encoder input is identical, but still recompute time/speaker boundary features and publish under the new transcript identity. Text corrections reuse only unchanged complete encoder inputs. With fixed groups of four turns, inserting one turn near the start can shift almost every later window; do not promise local re-embedding without measuring that effect. Evaluate stable derived-unit/block boundaries separately, under UNIT-03, before changing the representation to improve cache hits.

Coalesce simultaneous requests for the same exact key into one in-flight promise inside the inference owner. That is an efficiency mechanism, not durable job state. Eviction, worker termination and failed writes may lose the cache; they must not lose the recording's work request. Recheck identity when publishing results even on a cache hit. Keep caches recording-scoped initially to simplify deletion and avoid cross-recording membership leakage; any shared physical cache needs explicit ownership/reachability cleanup. A transferred `ArrayBuffer` detaches its sender's view: copy, reconstruct or transfer ownership deliberately instead of invalidating a cached array still in use.

**Decisive experiment:** replay a threshold-only change, timestamp edit, speaker edit, middle-word correction, inserted first turn and model/prefix change. Compare complete cold/warm output; record hit ratio, hash/tokenization cost, bytes read/written, avoided inference and eviction recovery. A cache wins only when avoided inference exceeds lookup/I/O/maintenance cost. This makes PERF-01 concrete without making PERF-02's production cache unconditional.

### TECH-04. Use a durable desired-analysis row and conditional result publication

The existing transcript, analysis, outcome and history stores share one IndexedDB database. That permits a narrow transactional solution to missed callbacks and stale overwrites; it does not require event sourcing or a generic workflow framework.

Introduce a proposed per-recording work row containing desired transcript generation/revision, required environment fingerprint, request epoch, scheduling/retry disposition and any claimed attempt token. Store a reference, not the full transcript. Final transcript `commit`/`replace` and the desired-work transition occur in the same library transaction. Live append alone does not start inference. When a hash is not yet available, the row records hash-pending input identity; compute it outside IndexedDB, then attach it only with an ID-04 identity check.

A dispatcher claims a bounded number of eligible rows and sends only admitted work to offscreen. Newer inputs supersede desired state rather than appending unbounded full transcripts to `AnalysisManager.pending`. Completion uses a library transaction to compare the attempt's captured input/environment/request epoch with the current transcript, latest desired state and deletion fence, write result/outcome, and retire that exact request. A stale completion may be acknowledged/discarded but cannot clear newer work or overwrite a newer published result. An explicit forced recompute advances the request epoch even when input bytes are unchanged.

The library work row and offscreen outbox are in different databases, so **do not claim an atomic transaction across them**. Keep at-least-once dispatch and acknowledgement-after-library-commit. A crash after claim but before send, after send but before job-ID recording, or after result commit but before acknowledgement must converge by reconciliation. An expired claim is permission to investigate/retry, not proof the old worker stopped: attempt fencing makes duplicate executions safe. Reconcile with live offscreen state, enforce retry/cancellation policy, and cap duplicate/in-flight bytes. Environment changes must update or reconcile desired rows; an old row alone is not proof that its environment remains current. Keep R3's read-time environment/input checks even with conditional publication. A due index accelerates ordinary recovery; R3's bounded full sweep remains a repair path for legacy rows and environment changes. [IndexedDB transaction semantics](https://www.w3.org/TR/IndexedDB-3/#transaction-concept)

**Acceptance:** inject termination at each boundary above; run A→B→C while A loads; replay A after C completes; force recomputation on unchanged input; cancel, delete and restart. Assert latest eligible input eventually completes, no durable request disappears before a matching result/explicit disposition, and neither stale outcomes nor stale attempts clear a newer row. This is a proposed cross-repository transaction seam and requires source changes plus schema migration in P0; it is not already implemented.

### TECH-05. Accelerate the existing weighted-SSE experiment with safe pruning

Use R6's Float64 prefix statistics and unpruned DP as the reference. For fixed vectors and positive weights, the cost satisfies:

```text
C(A∪B) − C(A) − C(B)
  = W_A*W_B/(W_A+W_B) * ||mean_A − mean_B||² ≥ 0.
```

This gives PELT's zero-constant pruning condition. With `D[0]=0` and a penalty per segment, predecessor a is dominated via endpoint s when `D[a]+C(a,s)>D[s]`, **but only at future b where the replacement segment (s,b) is legal**. Use strict comparison to retain ties unless a different tie policy is proved. Do not add another penalty into this dominance test; the final segment penalty cancels. [PELT theorem and conditions](https://arxiv.org/html/1101.1438)

Minimum duration/length requires delayed pruning. With minimum h units, domination at s cannot remove a for endpoints before `s+h`. A common maximum span L bounds cost to O(nLd); under compatible constraints a retained set can reduce actual work further. Worst case without a span bound remains quadratic in n, with vector-dimension cost; expected linear behavior is not established for these meetings. Boundary bonuses, candidate restrictions, data-dependent weights or a new cost require a fresh legality/pruning argument. Start with the plain SSE objective.

**Reproduced counterexample:** unit vectors `[-1,1,-1,1,-1,-1,1]`, unit weights, minimum length 2 and penalty 3/5. Correct DP returns endpoint `[7]` with cost `261/35`; immediate naive pruning returns `[4,7]` with cost `118/15`. At endpoint 6, its replacement for the deleted predecessor would require an illegal one-unit final segment. Keep this exact rational fixture and compare constrained pruning against exhaustive/unpruned solvers on small randomized cases. Count evaluated candidate segments and measure browser runtime; pruning is an exact acceleration of this challenger, not proof that SSE improves topic quality over current peaks.

### TECH-06. Build average-link topic hierarchies once and reuse their cuts

Make R6's global-clustering challenger precise. Normalize each nonzero temporal-segment leaf once; current segment means are not necessarily unit length. Begin with equal leaf masses to isolate linkage, then evaluate token/duration mass separately. For fixed unit leaves x_i and positive masses w_i:

```text
W_A = sum_(i∈A) w_i;  S_A = sum_(i∈A) w_i*x_i
averageCosine(A,B) = (S_A · S_B)/(W_A*W_B)
                  = mean_A · mean_B
```

Do **not** renormalize the cluster means. Their norms carry dispersion that `cosine(mean_A,mean_B)` removes. This is a different objective from the current first-qualifying centroid merge, not a drop-in preservation of it. For two clusters each containing vectors at ±20 degrees around the same direction, centroid cosine is 1, but average cross-pair cosine is about 0.883. Retune thresholds; `.93/.95` are not transferable.

For distance `d=1−averageCosine`, update `d(A∪B,C)=(W_A*d(A,C)+W_B*d(B,C))/(W_A+W_B)`. The update is constant time **per updated pair**. Equal masses give standard average linkage, not WPGMA's equal weighting of child clusters. Build a cubic global-minimum reference first; use a verified nearest-neighbor-chain implementation for O(m²) clustering after O(m²d) initial distances, where m counts temporal segments. A condensed Float64 distance table uses `8*m*(m−1)/2` bytes—about 3.81 MiB at m=1,000—before metadata. Bound m before allocating. [Linkage definitions and implementation bounds](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.cluster.hierarchy.linkage.html), [chain correctness and tie handling](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1109.2378)

Complete the hierarchy with proper merge sorting/relabeling, then reuse cuts for all candidate thresholds. Chain discovery order is not global merge-height order; do not stop the whole algorithm on its first above-threshold reciprocal pair. Reuse applies only while leaves, masses and metric stay fixed, and each partition still costs work to materialize/score. Average linkage does not guarantee that every within-topic pair matches; complete-link remains the stricter challenger if false merges persist.

**Checked:** explicit weighted-pair enumeration agrees with moment statistics on 1,000 seeded cases (maximum absolute error below 1.4e−16). Remaining acceptance: chain versus reference on tie-free cases, valid-minimum choices under ties, cancelling means, unequal masses, hierarchy cut equivalence, fixed-segmentation A→B→A recall and shared-vocabulary false merges. Avoid hidden reweighting by segment length during the first comparison.

### TECH-07. Test local token-state pooling as one new representation experiment

The late-chunking paper encodes context before pooling spans. Adapt that mechanism cautiously to **bounded local blocks** of the existing E5 encoder: retain token states in the embedding Worker, form prefix sums, then pool left/right spans around candidate boundaries at multiple widths. For a valid text-token span `[a,b)`, its mean is `(H_b−H_a)/(b−a)`; masked/special tokens need explicit exclusion and counts. Span extraction costs O(d) after prefix construction. Return compact vectors/scores and source mappings, not all meeting token states. This can trade many independent flank re-embeddings for one block forward pass. [Original late-chunking method and retrieval evaluation](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.04701v3)

