# Historical review — retained input, not current implementation status

The main conclusion is important:

> **You are not blocked on choosing an ASR model or obtaining your own transcript. There is a substantial architecture + NLP hardening phase that should happen before either.**
>
> More importantly, we can now run meaningful real-conversation research without your private transcript at all: public AMI/ICSI/QMSum data gives us actual meetings, speakers, timings and topic annotations. Your own Russian/Ukrainian calls remain necessary for the *final domain calibration*, but not for finding and fixing structural weaknesses.

## 1. State of the repository

PR #17's separation survived the later refactors well. The architecture is substantially healthier than it was when we first designed local processing:

```text
Content / capture
      │
      ▼
background control plane
      │
      ├── recording/*
      ├── library/transcript/*
      ├── library/analysis/*
      ├── runtime/*
      └── integrations/*
              │
              ▼
       canonical library state
              │
              ▼
offscreen data plane
      │
      ├── media / uploads
      └── analysis/
              │
              ▼
       dedicated embedding Worker

shared/analysis/*
      ↑
pure deterministic NLP
```

That division is good. In particular, I would **not rewrite the processing module into a new framework**.

The functional core/imperative shell separation is quite strong: vector maths, boundary detection, segmentation, clustering, keywords and importance live as pure code, while browser lifecycle, persistence and worker durability live outside it. Long-running inference remains in an offscreen-owned worker rather than an MV3 service worker. Background remains the canonical-data writer. Durable result acknowledgement is carefully handled. The later background split has also removed the old giant-controller shape.

The external integrations work is similarly well-shaped: there is one projection path from internal state into the public integration contract, privacy policy is explicit, internal IDs are not exported, and speaker pseudonymization is separated from canonical transcript state.

The current head's CI gate is successful. This particular run reused the repository's validation planner/cache and therefore skipped the expensive domain jobs rather than executing every suite afresh, so I would describe it as **CI gate green**, not “every test reran on this exact commit.”

### The current NLP pipeline

What presently ships is approximately:

```text
TranscriptSegment[]
       │
       ▼
4-utterance contextual windows
       │
       ▼
multilingual-e5-small Q8
384-dimensional normalized vectors
       │
       ├──── semantic boundary scores
       │       + pause
       │       + speaker change
       │       + discourse cue
       │
       ▼
local peaks
       │
       ▼
temporal segments
       │
       ▼
current-cluster assignment
       + periodic global centroid merges
       │
       ▼
global topics
       │
       ├── c-TF-IDF labels
       └── importance arithmetic
```

The underlying design remains attractive: deterministic, local, relatively cheap, easy to test, no large generative model, and reusable embeddings.

But several assumptions that made sense when PR #17 was designed have changed.

The biggest one is this:

> The clustering/segmentation architecture was consciously designed with “browser + incremental/live” constraints in mind, but the production trigger is now **post-recording batch analysis of a finished transcript**.

That changes what algorithms are affordable.

