# 2. The highest-priority problem is not NLP

There is one architectural problem I would fix **before introducing any STT engine**.

### An analysis currently does not identify the transcript that produced it

`AnalysisProvenance` records:

```ts
pipelineVersion
embeddingModel
embeddingModelRevision
embeddingDimensions
embeddingDtype
configHash
embeddingDevice? // diagnostic
```

It does **not** contain a transcript fingerprint/revision.

[Current analysis provenance implementation](https://github.com/kstroevsky/chrome-meeting-recording-extension/blob/feat/external-integrations-module/src/shared/analysis/provenance.ts?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

And `RecordingAnalysisCoordinator.analyze()` currently checks whether a current analysis already exists **before** reading the transcript:

```ts
if (!force && await analyses.get(historyId)) {
    return { ok: false, reason: 'already-analyzed' };
}
```

[RecordingAnalysisCoordinator](https://github.com/kstroevsky/chrome-meeting-recording-extension/blob/feat/external-integrations-module/src/background/library/analysis/RecordingAnalysisCoordinator.ts?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Today that is mostly harmless because Meet captions are effectively the one immutable transcript source.

It becomes a correctness bug the moment we have:

```text
recording
   ↓
transcript A
   ↓
analysis A

later:

STT / retranscription / imported transcript B
   ↓
???
```

The old analysis still has perfectly current:

```text
pipelineVersion = 2
model = E5
configHash = xyz
```

so the system cannot know it belongs to transcript A.

Worse, external integrations can eventually observe:

```text
transcript: B

analysis:
   topics computed from transcript A
```

and consider both ready.

The current integration snapshot code independently reads transcript and analysis, and stale analysis is mapped to `unsupported`; there is no cross-check tying the result to the transcript revision.

[IntegrationPreviewService](https://github.com/kstroevsky/chrome-meeting-recording-extension/blob/feat/external-integrations-module/src/background/integrations/IntegrationPreviewService.ts?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This should be fixed now, because it has nothing to do with which STT model wins.

## Proposed derived-data identity

I would introduce a stored transcript envelope conceptually like:

```ts
type StoredTranscript = {
  schemaVersion: 1;

  revision: number;
  contentHash: string;
  committedAt: number;

  transcript: Transcript;
};
```

The hash should cover the canonical normalized representation of:

```text
source
tStartMs
tEndMs
speaker
text
ordering
schemaVersion
```

For transcript identity I would use SHA-256/WebCrypto rather than reuse the 32-bit FNV config hash. This is not primarily a cryptographic-security requirement; it is a correctness identifier for arbitrary user content and can eventually cross persistence/integration boundaries.

Then:

```ts
type AnalysisProvenance = {
   ...
   transcriptHash: string;
   transcriptRevision: number;
};
```

Now the relationship becomes explicit:

```text
Transcript B
hash = H(B)

Analysis
input = H(B)
```

and staleness becomes:

```text
analysis.transcriptHash !== currentTranscript.contentHash
```

in addition to model/config/pipeline staleness.

This single addition unlocks clean retranscription, import, STT model switching, re-analysis and integration updates.

