# 5. A deeper NLP problem: “utterance” is currently source-defined

This is the biggest processing issue to solve before STT.

The current window builder says:

```text
4 transcript utterances = one semantic context
```

[Current contextual-window implementation](https://github.com/kstroevsky/chrome-meeting-recording-extension/blob/feat/external-integrations-module/src/shared/analysis/windows.ts?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

That implicitly assumes a `TranscriptSegment` has a reasonably stable linguistic granularity.

Meet captions may produce:

```text
one speaker turn
one revised caption
one phrase
```

A future ASR engine may instead produce:

```text
5-second chunk
sentence
VAD region
30-second segment
word-timestamp groups
```

Those are not interchangeable.

A pipeline whose fundamental semantic unit is “four upstream chunks” will change behavior when the ASR implementation changes even if **the words are identical**.

There is direct research support for treating this as a separate problem. Wan et al. showed that raw acoustically determined ASR segmentation does not necessarily match units appropriate for downstream NLP, and correcting the segmentation improved downstream translation and information retrieval. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="7"}

I would insert one layer:

```text
TranscriptSegment[]
        │
        ▼
AnalysisTurnNormalizer
        │
        ▼
AnalysisTurn[]
        │
        ▼
context windows
```

An `AnalysisTurn` is not another persisted canonical truth. It is a deterministic analysis representation.

Initially its production strategy can remain almost identity-like.

But the seam lets us later evaluate:

```text
same-speaker adjacent merge
token-budget turns
punctuation/sentence boundaries
VAD chunk joining
very-long-turn splitting
backchannel handling
```

without modifying `Transcript` or teaching the NLP pipeline about Parakeet/Qwen/GigaAM/Whisper.

### The test I care about most

Create semantically identical transcripts represented as:

```text
A: 30 speaker turns

B: same words split into 100 ASR-like chunks

C: same words merged into 15 long chunks
```

The *macro topics* should remain approximately invariant.

I suspect the current 4-utterance implementation will fail that test quite noticeably.

Finding that out **before** choosing ASR is extremely valuable.

