# 6. The newest research gives us a specific improvement to test right now

A particularly relevant 2026 paper appeared after PR #17 was designed: **“Unsupervised Topic Segmentation of Meetings with Length-Aware Semantic Weighting.”**

It addresses exactly a meeting-specific failure mode: short acknowledgements/backchannels such as “yeah”, “right”, “okay”, interruptions and low-information turns receiving too much influence. The authors add length-aware weighting to a global meeting segmentation approach and report consistent improvements on AMI, ICSI and AliMeeting4MUG under Pk and WindowDiff. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="8"}

This maps almost perfectly onto your current architecture.

Today:

```text
4 turns containing:

"Yeah."
"Right."
"Okay."
"I agree."

→ one embedding

and

4 dense 30-word turns
→ one embedding
```

Both become one equally weighted context vector downstream.

Likewise segment embeddings average window embeddings equally.

I would add an **experimental**, not shipping, aggregation strategy:

```text
uniform             // current baseline
token-count
sqrt(token-count)
log1p(token-count)
```

for window/segment semantic aggregation.

No new model is needed.

AMI/ICSI can tell us whether it helps.

This is one of the highest-value scientific experiments available to you today.

