# 7. We can stop waiting for your real transcript to benchmark segmentation

Your existing calibration harness is actually better than it was during PR #17.

It already understands:

```text
synthetic
real tuning
holdout
```

and does not tune on the holdout.

That is correct.

[Current calibration runner](https://github.com/kstroevsky/chrome-meeting-recording-extension/blob/feat/external-integrations-module/scripts/calibrate-analysis.ts?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

What is missing is actual public conversational ground truth.

## AMI is usable immediately

The original AMI corpus has **manual topic segmentation**, including hierarchical topic/subtopic labels and descriptions. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="10"}

There is also now the 2026 **AMI-ME** dataset: 130 AMI meetings and 2,459 fine-grained segments, with three effectiveness annotators per segment, under CC BY 4.0. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="11"}

One nuance: AMI-ME's fine-grained segmentation is partly generated/refined for its effectiveness task; I would **not treat that as a replacement for the original manually annotated AMI topic boundaries** without validating its construction. Use original AMI as the primary segmentation ground truth and AMI-ME as supplementary 2026 data.

ICSI is another real multi-speaker meeting corpus, and the public TreeSeg project already contains loaders for AMI and ICSI, which can save a lot of parsing work. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="12"}

QMSum is useful for a different property: it contains 232 meetings and `topic_list` entries with relevant spans, including non-contiguous spans for topics that recur later. That is very useful for testing your **temporal segments vs global recurring topics** design. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="13"}

So:

> **You do not need to wait for your private transcript to start validating the fundamental segmentation and recurrence mechanics.**

You still need your Russian/Ukrainian data before freezing production parameters, because AMI/ICSI are not your target language/domain.

