# 8. Your current evaluation metric is too narrow

The existing calibration's boundary score is effectively tolerant boundary F1 around ±1 window.

That was reasonable for a synthetic spike.

It is not enough for real algorithm selection.

Why?

With the current 4-turn window:

```text
±1 window
≈ ±4 utterances
```

Change the window size and the meaning of the metric itself changes.

I would report at least four complementary quantities:

| Aspect | Metric |
|---|---|
| exact/local boundary correctness | Boundary precision/recall/F1 |
| segmentation structure | Pk |
| segmentation structure | WindowDiff |
| playback UX | time-tolerant boundary F1, e.g. ±N seconds |
| global-topic assignment | pairwise P/R/F1 |
| cluster fairness | B³ or ARI |
| dangerous error | explicit false-merge count/rate |

Pk and WindowDiff remain standard in recent unsupervised segmentation papers, including *Words That Stick* and the 2026 meeting length-weighting paper. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="14"}

The time-based metric is particularly relevant because ACL 2026's *Beyond Transcripts* explicitly distinguishes transcript-space and **time-space** evaluation and shows that transcript quality and acoustic features affect chaptering. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="15"}

### Don't optimize one arithmetic average

Today you have a combined segmentation/clustering score.

I would not choose the eventual config by:

```text
(boundary F1 + cluster F1) / 2
```

alone.

A catastrophic false topic merge has a very different UX cost from a slight boundary displacement.

Instead, report a Pareto surface and impose constraints, for example:

```text
first:
false-merge rate <= acceptable limit

then maximize:
segmentation + clustering quality
```

And bootstrap scores **by meeting**, not by individual boundary, to get confidence intervals.

