# 10. We should reconsider local-peak-only segmentation

The current algorithm is approximately:

```text
adjacent window cosine change
        ↓
local peak detection
        ↓
min-duration repair
```

That is wonderfully simple.

But now that analysis happens **once after the conversation**, there is no need to restrict ourselves exclusively to local decisions.

A classic but still directly useful method is Content Vector Segmentation. It uses global optimization/dynamic programming over semantic coherence. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="18"}

This isn't merely academic historical trivia: Zoom's AutoMin 2023 system actually used CVS with sentence embeddings and dynamic programming for meeting segmentation. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="19"}

I would implement it as an experimental competitor over the **same cached E5 embeddings**.

Then compare:

```text
Current:
local peaks

Experiment:
global coherence + DP

Experiment:
global coherence + DP + length weighting
```

No new model.

No user transcript.

No product commitment.

That is exactly what a good research harness should allow.

### Two-stage boundary refinement is another inexpensive experiment

The current disjoint 4-turn windows solved the overlap-smearing problem, but they create coarse spatial resolution.

A useful compromise:

```text
coarse 4-turn disjoint scan
        ↓
candidate boundary region
        ↓
re-embed only that local region
with smaller disjoint left/right contexts
        ↓
refined utterance-level boundary
```

You only pay extra embeddings around a handful of candidates.

That deserves a benchmark.

