# 11. The global clustering algorithm deserves a challenger

This is another place where the post-recording trigger changes the tradeoff.

Current behavior is roughly:

```text
new temporal segment
   ↓
compare to current cluster
   ↓
join / open cluster
   ↓
periodic centroid merge
```

It is cheap and incremental.

But it has an asymmetric error model:

```text
false split
→ may be repaired by later merge

false join
→ cannot be repaired
```

It is also order-dependent.

For a completed meeting with, say, tens of temporal segments, a full similarity matrix is tiny:

```text
100 segments
→ 10,000 cosine values
```

That's nothing compared with embedding inference.

I would therefore benchmark a global clustering challenger:

```text
segment embeddings
      ↓
full cosine matrix
      ↓
average-link / complete-link agglomeration
      ↓
threshold
```

and optionally a post-hoc **split low-cohesion cluster** pass.

I would prefer average/complete linkage over connected-components/single-link behavior because chaining can create exactly the overmerge problem we're trying to avoid.

Recent work reinforces the importance of richer contextual representations for dialogue theme clustering. CATCH 2025 focuses on context-aware representations because sparse, short dialogue utterances cluster poorly in isolation. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="20"}

S³ 2025 is also worth watching for global semantic structure: it uses ICA in embedding space and reports substantially faster contextual topic modeling than BERTopic. It is **not a drop-in meeting segmentation algorithm**, but it is relevant as a possible future global-topic challenger. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="21"}

I would not ship either now.

I would make the harness capable of comparing them.

