# 18. ASR noise can already be simulated

We can build robustness testing before choosing Parakeet/GigaAM/Qwen/etc.

Take a clean public meeting transcript and generate controlled corruption:

```text
word deletion
content-word substitution
function-word substitution
repetition
filler insertion
punctuation removal
speaker-label removal
utterance merging
utterance splitting
timestamp jitter
```

Then measure:

```text
boundary degradation
false topic merges
cluster F1 degradation
label stability
```

This creates curves such as:

```text
5% semantic corruption  → cluster F1 -2%
10%                     → -7%
20%                     → -23%
```

That tells us how robust the processing system itself is.

And later, when comparing ASR models, **this downstream degradation matters more than WER alone**.

Recent research strongly supports this. *WER is Unaware* found that conventional ASR metrics correlated poorly with the actual semantic impact of errors in dialogue. Its domain is clinical dialogue, so the exact metric does not transfer to meetings, but the methodological lesson does. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="25"}

Likewise, work on correcting ASR segmentation found downstream tasks improve when segmentation errors are corrected even without changing the core recognizer. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="26"}

Therefore the eventual ASR bake-off should report:

```text
WER/CER
+
topic boundary degradation
+
topic cluster degradation
+
keyword/label degradation
```

not merely WER.

