# 23. Research approaches I explicitly would **not** build now

There are several impressive recent methods that I investigated but would defer.

M³Seg directly targets unsupervised topic segmentation of ASR transcripts and reports large gains, but introduces learned region/edge machinery. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="30"}

UPS 2025 models utterance-pair adjacency/topic affiliation and performs strongly under several supervision regimes, but requires another trained model. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="31"}

SegNSP 2026 frames boundaries as next-sentence continuation and reports B-F1 0.79 on CitiLink-Minutes and 0.65 on WikiSection, 0.17 above its reproducible baseline on the latter. It is scientifically very relevant—but again means another fine-tuned encoder. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="32"}

Summarization-based segmentation also performs well on noisy spoken dialogue, but it reintroduces a generative dependency you deliberately removed. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="33"}

These papers should influence the **research interfaces**—especially making boundary scoring pluggable enough to compare pair-coherence scores later—but not the v1 production dependency graph.

