# Final assessment

The processing subsystem from PR #17 was worth building. Its strongest choice was not any particular cosine threshold; it was the separation:

```text
transcript contract
      ↓
pure deterministic semantic core
      ↓
worker-backed inference
      ↓
versioned derived result
```

That has held up through 172 subsequent commits and a major background restructuring.

The weaknesses are now mostly at the **input/derived-data boundary** and in the **scientific validation layer**, not the browser runtime architecture.

The single most important correction is:

```text
Analysis must be derived from
a specific immutable transcript revision,
not merely from "recording ID".
```

The single most important NLP improvement is:

```text
Raw TranscriptSegment
must stop implicitly meaning
"stable semantic utterance".
```

And the biggest new opportunity is that **we do not have to wait for your transcripts to do serious research anymore**. AMI/ICSI plus recent meeting-segmentation work gives us enough real conversational structure to test the architecture scientifically now. Original AMI provides manually segmented meetings; recent research demonstrates useful directions like global optimization, length-aware weighting, lexical/keyword cohesion and richer dialogue features. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="34"}

Your own Russian/Ukrainian recordings are still the thing that eventually answers:

> “Are these the right final settings for **this product**?”

But they are **not** the thing preventing us from answering:

> “Is this a robust, correctly versioned, source-independent and scientifically competitive processing architecture?”

We can answer the second question—and implement most of the improvements—now.